SEO training works when the team uses its own site, data and decisions during the session. Generic slides explain terminology. They don't show a writer which page is competing with another page or show a developer which template is creating the crawl problem.

I deliver live training for marketing teams, content teams, developers, agency staff and founders. Every workshop has a curriculum and practical work. Attendees leave with methods they can apply to the site they already manage.

## Who SEO Training Is For

SEO training is for teams that need repeatable skills rather than another list of recommendations. In-house marketing teams use it to connect search demand with campaigns and commercial priorities. Content writers use it to plan pages, match intent and edit copy against evidence.

Developers use the technical modules to understand crawlability, indexation, rendering, canonicals and Core Web Vitals in the context of their own templates. Agency juniors use the programme to move beyond tool exports and explain why a finding matters.

Founders doing their own SEO need a narrower route. Training helps them identify the work that can be handled internally and the point where specialist support will save time or reduce risk. The guide to [in-house SEO versus an agency or consultant](/blog/inhouse-seo-vs-agency-vs-consultant/) explains the operating models before a team commits to one.

The programme assumes attendees know their business. I bring the search process and 15+ years of practical SEO work. The sessions combine those two sources instead of replacing sector knowledge with a standard example.

## What the Training Covers

SEO training covers five core modules. The emphasis changes with the team's role and current level, but every module uses the attendee's own site and data.

**Search fundamentals and intent.** Attendees learn how different queries reflect research, comparison and action. The exercises map real queries to existing pages and show where several pages compete for the same intent.

**Technical SEO essentials.** The team checks crawl paths, indexation, status codes, canonicals, sitemaps, rendering and Core Web Vitals. Developers can work at template level while marketers learn enough to describe a fault accurately.

**Semantic content and topical maps.** Writers and strategists learn how to connect a core service with supporting questions and related attributes. The practical task builds part of a topical map and turns it into a page brief. My guide to [becoming an SEO](/blog/how-to-be-an-seo/) gives attendees a wider skills framework.

**Google Search Console and GA4 reporting.** Attendees find query, page and device changes in Google Search Console and connect them with relevant GA4 actions. The module focuses on useful decisions rather than a dashboard of unexplained numbers.

**AI search visibility.** The team learns how answer engines extract passages, compare sources and cite useful pages. Exercises review answer structure, evidence and brand mentions without treating AI traffic as a replacement for commercial measurement. The [ChatGPT prompts for SEO](/blog/chatgpt-prompts-for-seo/) guide provides practical follow-up work.

## How Training Is Delivered

Training is delivered as a three-hour half-day workshop, a six-hour full-day workshop or a six-week team programme. Workshops can run remotely or on site. The six-week programme uses one 90-minute live session per week with set work between sessions.

Remote delivery works well for distributed teams and screen-based technical work. On-site workshops suit teams that want longer group exercises or cross-department discussion. Each format is capped at 8 people so attendees have time to work through their own examples.

Sessions can be recorded with the team's agreement. Attendees receive the materials, exercise templates and a summary of the work completed. A follow-up question and answer session resolves issues that appear when the team applies the method after training.

The six-week programme provides more implementation time. Each week builds on the last. The team can map demand, inspect the site, draft a brief, publish a change and return with evidence rather than trying to absorb the whole process in one day.

